From c3a5c0e5148ad1528cc02c757b302a144fa82fd7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Torra <n770galaxy@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:47:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] ##arch: Add USSE and PDS assemblers

Both decoders now come from a shared, clean-room table of instruction
forms that also encodes, so assembling is the inverse of disassembling:
any text the decoder prints assembles back to a word that decodes the
same.
- usse: sync the core (usse.c plus the form engine usse_form.c and the
  instruction families in forms/) and add the encode callback.
- pds: add the PDS forms (pds_forms.c) and the encode callback. They run
  on the USSE form engine, built once in the usse plugin; arch_pds.so
  links it in. r_asm strips a leading '(' before encoding, so the plugin
  restores it on predicated instructions such as "(p0) shl ...".
- Matching is case-insensitive, as r_asm lowercases its input.
- test/db/asm: the USSE entries and the canonical PDS words now test
  both directions; PDS words with ignored bits set stay disassembly
  tests, next to their canonical form.
